# Museum ARVR Performance Optimization Report

## Executive Summary

The Virtual Museum ARVR application has been optimized for smooth desktop navigation, higher frame rates (stable 60 FPS), fast initial page load times, and low GPU/CPU memory consumption—without altering the visual identity, changing WASD movement, or removing any of the 15 integrated museum artifacts.

---

## 1. Root Causes of Lag (Diagnosis)

1. **Per-Frame React State Triggers**: `MuseumControls.jsx` previously called `onCameraMove([x, y, z])` on every frame inside `useFrame`, triggering a `setState` on the root `Home` component **60 times per second**. This forced the entire React tree and 3D prop hierarchy to re-evaluate on every camera step.
2. **Simultaneous GLB Load Parsing**: Mounting all 15 GLB model loaders simultaneously inside the R3F Canvas forced WebGL to parse ~158 MB of binary GLB data at once on startup.
3. **Uncached Bounding Box & Scene Cloning**: `AutoFitModel.jsx` executed a deep recursive `.clone(true)` on every render and explicitly set `child.frustumCulled = false` on every mesh, preventing the GPU from culling off-screen polygons.
4. **Uncapped Device Pixel Ratio (DPR)**: High-DPI screens (3x/4x mobile displays) rendered at native resolution, forcing 9x pixel shader workload per frame.

